# __APP_TITLE__

__APP_TITLE__ is a Gin and GORM backend split into a **local** (on-site) service and a **cloud**
service that share one set of entities and helpers.

Both services ship with PostgreSQL persistence, Swagger annotations, OpenTelemetry traces/logs/metrics,
slog request and GORM logging, cobra commands and uber/dig dependency injection. The cloud service adds
JWT authentication with users, roles and user-roles. S3, Infisical and NATS are included in the codebase
but commented out; see the `apta-go-local-cloud` skill (`references/optional-modules.md`) to enable them.

## Getting started

Run these from `local/` or `cloud/` (each has its own `.env`):

```bash
cp example.env .env      # configure database, OTLP (and JWT on cloud) settings
make swag                # generate swagger docs (docs/ is git-ignored)
make migrate             # migrate the database (cloud also seeds the default admin user)
make go                  # run the API (or `make dev` for live reload with air)
```

Swagger UI: local `http://localhost:__LOCAL_PORT__/swagger/index.html`, cloud `http://localhost:__CLOUD_PORT__/swagger/index.html`.

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `make swag` | Regenerate swagger docs |
| `make dev` | Live reload with `air` |
| `make go` | Run the API server |
| `make migrate` | Migrate the database (cloud: and seed the default user) |
| `make migrate-force` | Drop all tables, then migrate |
| `make test` | Run this module's tests (shared tests: `go test ./shared/...`) |
| `make secret` | Print a random value for `JWT_SECRET` |

## Workspace layout

`go.work` ties three modules together:

| Module | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `shared/` | Entities, enums, common helpers, `pkg/*`. Depends only on third-party packages, never on `local` or `cloud`. |
| `local/` | Local (on-site) service. Open API (no authn/authz) because it runs on the site network; announces itself via mDNS (`__MDNS_TYPE__`). Runs on port __LOCAL_PORT__ by default. |
| `cloud/` | Cloud service: authn/authz on, schema-scoped reads (`?scope=<site>`, sites listed at `GET /api/v1/sites`). Runs on port __CLOUD_PORT__ by default. |

Each module's `go.mod` also carries a `replace` for `../shared` so `go mod tidy` and Docker builds work
without the workspace. Build images from the repo root: `docker build -f local/Dockerfile .` and
`docker build -f cloud/Dockerfile .`.

## How data moves between local and cloud

Each site runs its own **local** service with its own PostgreSQL database. A single **cloud** service
gives head office a combined view of every site. Every table has exactly one **authority**: the side where
that data is created and edited. The other side only holds a read-only copy.

| Table | Authority (edit here) | Copy lives on | Direction |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
<!-- scaffold:authority -->

Add rows with `scaffold.py crud --authority local|cloud`; the generator exposes write endpoints only on the
authority side and `GET` endpoints on the other.

### Where each site's data lands in the cloud

The cloud database keeps its own data in the main schema (`DB_SCHEMA`, default `public`) and one extra
schema per site holding that site's replicated rows. Reads on the cloud API pick one with the `scope` query
parameter:

| Request | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /api/v1/sites` | The list of site schemas |
| `GET /api/v1/<resource>` | Main schema (no `scope`) |
| `GET /api/v1/<resource>?scope=<site>` | That site's rows |

An unknown `scope` is rejected with `400`. `scope` is only supported on `GET`; writes always go to the main schema.

### Good to know

- The sync is done at the database level (PeerDB), not by application code. Neither service calls the other
  over HTTP, and the replication setup lives outside this repository. Tables whose authority is the cloud get
  PeerDB bookkeeping columns on the local database during `migrate`.
- To add a new table, decide its authority first. It decides which side may write, and which direction the
  replication must run.
